<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter of Cantabria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Peter, Duke of Cantabria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a> was a duke of Cantabria in the early 8th century, known only from the Asturian chronicles of the late 9th century, which claim he descended from the Visigothic kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liuvigild">Leovigild</a> and Reccared. His son Alfonso married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermesinda">Ermesinda</a>, daughter of Pelagius, and became King Alfonso I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> in 739, uniting Cantabria with the Asturian kingdom.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Peter, Petrus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Pedro in Spanish, was an eighth-century Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a>. Various writers have tried to name his parentage, making him a son or brother of King Erwig, but the early sources say nothing more specific than the chronicle of Pseudo-Alfonso, which calls him descended from the bloodline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liuvigild">Liuvigild</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reccared-i">Reccared I</a>. Even this has been challenged as a possible politically motivated fiction, created to support his descendants' later claim to exclusive kingship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to the Muslim chroniclers, Musa ibn Nusair sacked Amaya, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a>, in 714, a year after Tariq had done so. Peter, the provincial dux, led his people to refuge in the mountains. When the local noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagius-of-asturias">Pelayo</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> rebelled against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> garrison set over the mountain passes of neighbouring Asturias, Peter and other western Galician nobles gave their support against the common enemy. Some scholars have suggested that Pelayo may have been a Cantabrian relative, defending the western access to the duchy from his own county seat.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-covadonga">Battle of Covadonga</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagius-of-asturias">Pelayo</a> avoided defeat by a larger invading force and drove its governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munuza">Munuza</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, Peter seems likely to have sent his son to Pelayo's court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cangas-de-onis">Cangas de Onís</a>. Sending noble children to the royal court was a Visigothic practice, so the act amounted to a tacit admission of Pelayo's regality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Crónica Albeldense records the marriage that joined the two leaders' lands, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a> and the rest of what would be called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> merged into one realm. Peter's son Alfonso later succeeded to the Asturian throne and was the first to use the title of king. His other son, Fruela of Cantabria, was the father of the kings Aurelius and Bermudo I. Iberian Muslim scholars called the line the Beni Alfons; it is more commonly known as the Astur-Leonese dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter of Cantabria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The throne then passed to Alfonso I, son of Duke <b>Peter of Cantabria</b> and husband of Pelagius’s daughter Ermesinda, which united Asturias and Cantabria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Muslim conquest of 711, Duke <b>Peter of Cantabria</b>, known only from the Asturian chronicles, probably kept some independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-cantabria">Duchy of Cantabria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.33,-4&amp;year=764" aria-label="Cantabria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Peter of Cantabria"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4751 3866h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4751 3866h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.33,-4&amp;year=764">What was here in 764 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
